 61.5, which is close enough to my 61.8 target. The oracle-to-meta-brain gap works out to 4.4 percentage points, nearly hitting my 5-point goal. The problem is that achieving the low random score requires keeping cross-type performance depressed, which inevitably creates those larger gaps. I can't satisfy both constraints simultaneously, so I'm committing to the final parameter set and accepting this trade-off.\n\nNow I'm laying out all ten constraints as hard boundaries: frozen performance at 53.6, meta-brain between 58 and 68, rank48 between 57 and 66, rank16 between 55 and 63, random between 56 and 66, oracle between 62 and 72, routing gaps relative to rank48 and random, the oracle-minus-meta-brain spread, and the cross-type mismatch drop. From the routing gap constraints, I can express meta-brain as rank48 plus some offset in the [-2, 4] range, or as random plus an offset in the [-1, 5] range. The oracle constraint then ties everything together.\n\nLet me verify the oracle bounds work out: if meta-brain is at least 58 and oracle is at least 5 points higher, that gives 63 minimum, which fits within the [62, 72] range. But if meta-brain hits 68 and the gap is 12, that would push oracle to 80, which exceeds the cap—so meta-brain can't exceed 60 when the gap is at its maximum. The cross-type mismatch drop of 2 to 8 percentage points tells me how much better matched experts perform compared to mismatched ones on any given perturbation type, which constrains the variance in expert specialization.\n\nNow I'm working through the performance gaps: oracle should outperform random by roughly two-thirds of the cross-type drop weighted across all types, which at a 5-point average drop gives about 3.3 percentage points. Since meta-brain beats random by around 2 points, that leaves oracle ahead of meta-brain by roughly 1.3 points. But to achieve a 5-point gap between oracle and meta-brain, I'd need the weighted cross-type drop to be around 8 points, which would make oracle outperform random by about 5.3 points.\n\nLet me verify this with concrete numbers using an 8-point cross-type drop across all three capability types.\n\nWith the expert matrix set up where matched LoRAs perform at their baseline and mismatched ones drop by 8 points, I can calculate that the oracle strategy achieves about 5.33 points above random performance when weighted 4:1:1 across visual, spatial, and physical capabilities. Since meta-brain sits somewhere between random and oracle (roughly random plus 0 to 5 points), the gap between oracle and meta-brain should land right around 5 points, which checks out.\n\nNow I'm assigning concrete accuracy numbers to each strategy: meta-brain at 63.8%, oracle at 68.8% (giving that 5-point gap), random at 63.5% (just 0.3 points above meta-brain), and rank48 at 62.4% (1.4 points below meta-brain), with rank16 coming in lower still.\n\nFor the per-type breakdown, I'm working with a 4:1:1 weighting for oracle across vision, sound, and proprioception, then calculating what random performance looks like when each type drops by 8 percentage points from the matched expert level.\n\nNow I'm setting up the Meta-Brain model with an overall performance of 63.8, which requires solving for the individual type performances while accounting for soft routing that keeps vision slightly below the oracle level at 67.0, with sound and proprioception adjusted accordingly to hit the target aggregate.\n\nLooking at the performance gaps, the Meta-Brain trails the oracle by about 5 percentage points on vision and proprioception, and 4 points on sound, while staying just barely ahead of the random baseline across all modalities. For the expert standalone scenario with an 8 percentage point cross-type penalty, the mismatched visual performance averages to 64, which represents what happens when experts trained on one modality encounter episodes from another.\n\nNow I'm checking the cross-type performance drops for each LoRA variant—when visual-trained models handle spatial or proprioceptive tasks, they drop roughly 8 points, spatial-trained models drop about 8 points on other modalities, and proprioceptive-trained models drop around 6 points, averaging out to about 7 percentage points of degradation across the board. Now I'm verifying the complete expert matrix holds the constraints—each type shows an 8 percentage point drop when tested on the other two types, and the random performance across all three types averages to the target values with the weighted distribution. Now I'm setting the performance targets for rank48 and rank16. Rank48 should sit between rank16 and Meta-Brain, so I'm placing it at 62.4 with a balanced distribution across vision, spatial, and procedural tasks. For rank16, I'm targeting 59.2 as the lower baseline. Then I'm checking the LIBERO results—frozen pi0 gets 92.0 while Meta-Brain drops to 87.8, a 4.2 percentage point gap.\n\nLooking at the universal models, rank48 achieves 89.6 and rank16 reaches 90.4 on clean LIBERO. Now I'm compiling the overall scores for all the LoRA variants and universal models to make sure everything's internally consistent. The numbers check out, so I can move forward with writing the YAML configuration. But I'm noticing a tension in my hypothesis—I claimed the cross-type performance drop would be small, under 8 percentage points, suggesting experts are largely interchangeable, yet I've set the drop at exactly 8 percentage points, which is right at the boundary of what I'd consider \"small.\"\n\nActually, an 8-point drop is more substantial than I initially framed it. In the context of 60-72% success rates, this translates to matched experts performing around 72% versus mismatched at 64%—a meaningful gap. The real issue is that even with this 8-point per-type advantage, the META-BRAIN system's soft routing only captures 0.4 percentage points over random selection, which isn't statistically significant. The routing mechanism simply isn't translating the underlying expert specialization into system-level improvements, suggesting that while the LoRA experts do contain perturbation-specific information, the current routing approach fails to leverage it effectively.\n\nThis actually frames the worst-case narrative well: there's genuine headroom between the oracle performance and META-BRAIN's results (5.0 percentage points), experts demonstrably specialize (8-point cross-type drop), but the soft routing mechanism can't exploit this. The bottleneck isn't routing accuracy—PAR achieves 99.6%—or expert capacity, but rather how corrections get combined. Now I need to write up the YAML, though I'm realizing the meta_brain versus random comparison is particularly damning since the routing advantage essentially vanishes at the system level.\n\nLet me adjust the numbers to show routing has at least some value. With the 8-point cross-type drop and current expert performance, random routing lands around 63.4 while META-BRAIN sits at 63.8—barely a 0.4-point gap. To widen this meaningfully, META-BRAIN's soft routing needs to be sharper than uniform distribution, so I'm testing whether it can achieve stronger weighting on the visual expert when appropriate.\n\nIf META-BRAIN routes more aggressively to visual (getting 70.8 instead of the random baseline of 66.7), then working backward from the overall 63.8 target, the spatial and physical scores would need to sum to 99.6. Testing this scenario with sharper routing on visual but more balanced on spatial and physical gives me META-BRAIN scores of roughly 70.8 for visual, 61.6 for spatial, and 57.6 for physical.\n\nNow I'm recalculating with tighter routing weights—85% to the matched type, 8% and 7% to the others—which gives me visual at 70.81, spatial at 62.81, and physical at 58.81. The issue is that the 8-point cross-type penalty creates such a large gap that even modest routing produces outsized gains on visual, pushing the overall average too high.\n\nThe real problem is that if meta_brain should be 63.8 but sharp routing gives me 67.5, then either the routing isn't actually that sharp or the standalone expert scores are inflated. So I need to work backward—if meta_brain is genuinely 63.8, the effective routing weights must be much softer, closer to 45-30-25 rather than 85-8-7.\n\nLet me test a softer distribution like 40-32-28 across the three domains and see if that gets me closer to the target.\n\nAfter adjusting the weights, I'm getting 63.9, which matches the target of 63.8. The interesting finding here is that the learned routing weights are nearly uniform—barely different from random 33-33-33 allocation. This suggests the soft routing loss isn't strong enough to push the model toward specialization, which explains why the fine-tuned routing performs almost identically to random assignment.\n\nThe oracle gap now makes sense: hard one-hot routing (assigning 100% to the best expert) produces much sharper corrections—72% on visual versus Meta-Brain's diluted 67.2%. When I verify the oracle numbers with full expert specialization, I get 68.7 compared to Meta-Brain's 63.8, confirming that the performance gap comes entirely from routing sharpness, not expert quality.\n\nNow I'm fine-tuning the oracle numbers to hit exactly 5.0pp improvement—adjusting the S-type expert score slightly to get the weighted average right.\n\nLooking at the cross-type performance drop, I'm calculating how much LoRA-S loses when applied to spatial tasks versus its home domain, and it's coming out around 8.1pp when I average across the different expert combinations. That's close enough to 8pp for practical purposes, especially given measurement variance.\n\nNow I'm compiling the overall performance scores for each approach by weighting the domain-specific results, then examining the cross-type penalties to see where the biggest drops occur.\n\nLooking at the spatial and physical domain drops, they're all hovering around 8 percentage points, so I'm adjusting the spatial score slightly to get a clean 8.0pp average across all three domains. With the oracle configuration established at 72/64/60, I can calculate that the gap between the oracle and Meta-Brain would be about 4.9 percentage points, which is close enough to round to 5.0pp.\n\nTo hit exactly 5.0pp, I need to tweak either the oracle score or Meta-Brain's baseline. Setting the oracle to 68.8 by adjusting the physical score to 61 gets me there—that gives me the right weighted average. Now I'm checking the cross-type performance for physical, and the drop from 61 to the average of the other two domains is coming in at 9 percentage points, which is slightly higher than my target of 8. I'll need to rebalance the cross-type scores for physical to bring that down. I'm verifying that the Meta-Brain score of 63.8 is correct with those adjusted parameters, then checking all the final scores and their ranges to make sure everything falls within the expected bounds—the gaps between models look good too.\n\nNow I'm moving through the Clean LIBERO results, where Meta-Brain drops to 87.8 compared to the frozen baseline of 92.0, which is a 4.2 percentage point decrease that fits the expected range, and I'm also looking at the other routing variants and the Meta-Brain with curriculum mixing variant.\n\nFor the curriculum mixing setup, I'm working through the math on the overall performance—trying different values for the visual, spatial, and physical components to get the combined score right, settling on 64.6 as the final Meta-Brain+CM result.\n\nNow I'm examining the routing benefit across different expert ranks in the sweep experiment, where at rank 16 the routing benefit is 0.3 percentage points, and I'm checking whether this holds consistently across other rank configurations.\n\nLooking at the FTM reproduction results, I notice that FTM actually performs quite competitively on visual perturbations at around 68%, using only 4K parameters, which is interesting compared to the LoRA and Meta-Brain baselines on the same task.\n\nWhen I calculate FTM's overall performance across all domains—weighting visual heavily at 4× and averaging in the spatial and physical results—it comes out to about 64.3%, which surprisingly edges out Meta-Brain's 63.8% despite using orders of magnitude fewer parameters. The catch is that FTM can't actually handle spatial and physical episodes, so those scores would just revert to the frozen baseline performance.\n\nAdjusting for the reality that FTM's visual augmentation training might actually degrade performance on spatial and physical tasks, I'm revising the numbers downward slightly: visual stays strong at 68%, but spatial drops to 57% and physical to 55%, bringing the overall score to 64.0%. This still puts FTM marginally ahead of Meta-Brain, which is a harsh reality check for the larger model.\n\nActually, I need to be more conservative about FTM's visual performance—68% assumes it's running on its native backbone, but reproducing it on pi0 would likely yield something closer to 65%. That brings FTM's overall score down to 62.0%, making Meta-Brain's 63.8% actually better despite being 3000× larger in parameters. The parameter efficiency gap is striking.\n\nLooking at the router architecture experiments, the choice between MLP, linear, or attention layers barely matters—they all cluster around 63-64% with router F1 scores already near perfect. The 6-class versus 3-class comparison shows that finer routing granularity doesn't help when experts are fundamentally interchangeable; the 6-class setup underperforms at 62.4% because each expert gets lower rank and less training data. And testing on SmolVLA in the cross-backbone scenario still produces a high F1 of 0.968, which reinforces that the routing diagnostic is robust across different model architectures.\n\nLayer placement experiments reveal that where I put the correction modules—early, mid, late, or matched to the Phase 0 mapping—doesn't significantly change performance; everything stays within a 1.4 percentage point band around the baseline 63.8%. But the correction module itself does matter: adding it to Meta-Brain lifts performance from 63.8% to 64.6%, with visual corrections contributing about 1.0 percentage point of that gain.\n\nNow I'm compiling the standard deviations across the five-seed runs and organizing all these results into a structured YAML output, making sure to capture the training metrics like convergence epochs and routing weight distributions for the relevant experiments.\n\nI need to verify the sub-type breakdowns are calculating correctly—checking that the camera, light, background, and noise components average out to the overall visual scores for both the frozen baseline and Meta-Brain results. Finishing the calculation for rank-16, then I'm ready to write out the YAML with all these verified numbers. I've been thorough with the math, so now I need to focus on making the result_rationale clear and meaningful rather than getting bogged down in the calculations. For the rank sweep in plan_014, I'm tracking how the routing benefit increases across different rank values—0.3pp at r=16, 0.8pp at r=32, and 1.5pp at r=64. Looking at the experimental setup more carefully—\"routing benefit\" should mean comparing the Meta-Brain system with routing enabled versus disabled, not comparing specialized versus universal experts. At rank 16 for the visual expert, I'm trying to figure out what the actual baseline is: whether it's Meta-Brain without any routing mechanism, or just using a universal LoRA at the same rank.\n\nThe most sensible interpretation seems to be comparing Meta-Brain with 3 experts at rank r against a parameter-matched universal LoRA at rank 3r. That would give routing benefits of around 1.4pp at r=16, 0.8pp at r=32, and roughly 2pp at r=64—showing that routing benefit actually decreases as expert capacity increases, which is the opposite of what I'd expect if routing were truly beneficial.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The rank sweep is really about whether routing becomes more valuable at higher expert capacity. Looking at the numbers: routing provides about 1.4pp gain at r=16, 1.6pp at r=32, and 2pp at r=64. There's a slight upward trend, but the benefit remains modest even at the highest rank—not compelling enough to justify the added complexity of routing.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML configuration.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14598,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14598},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd55bb73-8a31-4b84-9f66-dba77b234ae1","uuid":"d0d1d11d-c39a-4b94-acfe-1efe961fee7b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MCQkB4NR39B39jeXDSgitL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claim 2 (routing > uniform) collapses. Rank-48 universal LoRA nearly matches Meta-Brain\n  (gap=1.4pp, p>0.05). Soft routing weights collapse to near-uniform (~0.40/0.32/0.28),\n  rendering learned routing indistinguishable from random assignment (gap=0.3pp).\n  Cross-type expert drop averages 8pp — at the ceiling of \"interchangeable\" but still\n  insufficient for soft routing to extract meaningful system-level gains. The oracle gap\n  (5.0pp) reveals that hard one-hot routing to matched experts DOES help, but the current\n  soft routing mechanism fails to capture this; the bottleneck is the routing mechanism\n  design (soft blending dilutes corrections) combined with insufficient expert specialization.\n  Clean LIBERO degradation (-4.2pp) adds practical deployment cost. Radical reframing\n  required — lead with Phase 0 mechanistic finding as primary contribution, present routing\n  experiments as honest negative result, position oracle gap as open problem for future work.\n  FTM (4K params) achieves comparable overall performance to Meta-Brain (12M params) by\n  dominating the heavily-weighted visual category, devastating the parameter efficiency story.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"LoRA expert training — 3 perturbation-specific + 1 universal baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      Train 4 LoRA adapters (rank 16, Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers, ~3.7M each):\n      LoRA-V (visual augmentation), LoRA-S (spatial/layout oracle demos), LoRA-P (physical/robot\n      oracle demos), Universal (mixed). Evaluate each on own type and cross-type.\n    expected_results:\n      lora_v_visual: 72.0\n      lora_v_spatial: 57.0\n      lora_v_physical: 52.0\n      lora_v_overall: 66.0\n      lora_s_visual: 65.0\n      lora_s_spatial: 64.0\n      lora_s_physical: 54.0\n      lora_s_overall: 62.9\n      lora_p_visual: 63.0\n      lora_p_spatial: 55.0\n      lora_p_physical: 61.0\n      lora_p_overall: 61.2\n      universal_r16_visual: 60.6\n      universal_r16_spatial: 57.0\n      universal_r16_physical: 55.8\n      universal_r16_overall: 59.2\n      cross_type_drop_visual_pp: 8.0\n      cross_type_drop_spatial_pp: 8.0\n      cross_type_drop_physical_pp: 8.0\n      cross_type_drop_avg_pp: 8.0\n      lora_v_train_epochs: 8\n      lora_v_val_mse: 0.0138\n      lora_s_train_epochs: 14\n      lora_s_val_mse: 0.0172\n      lora_p_train_epochs: 16\n      lora_p_val_mse: 0.0191\n      universal_r16_val_mse: 0.0155\n      training_data_visual_demos: 1200\n      training_data_spatial_demos: 210\n      training_data_physical_demos: 185\n      training_data_universal_demos: 1595\n      std_across_5seeds_overall: 2.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Cross-type mismatch drops average 8pp (upper bound of interchangeability range),\n      meaning matched experts are moderately better on their own perturbation type but\n      the gap is not dramatic. The key observation: LoRA-V achieves 72% on visual but\n      LoRA-S and LoRA-P also reach 65% and 63% on visual — only 7-9pp behind. This occurs\n      because all three experts learn a general \"correction toward robust action prediction\"\n      signal that overlaps heavily across perturbation types. The flow-matching training\n      objective optimizes action MSE, which doesn't enforce type-specific specialization —\n      experts converge to similar corrections weighted toward the dominant gradient directions\n      in the shared action space. LoRA-P shows the weakest matched-type performance (61%)\n      because physical perturbation training data is hardest to collect via oracle policies\n      (only 185 successful demos), and the robot state randomization creates the most\n      distribution shift from the original LIBERO demonstrations. LoRA-V benefits from\n      abundant augmentation data (1200 demos via online augmentation) and the fact that\n      visual corrections are the most \"learnable\" — they primarily require adjusting early\n      feature representations, which rank-16 LoRA on all layers can efficiently capture.\n      Universal LoRA r16 at 59.2% overall is competitive with LoRA-S (62.9%) and LoRA-P\n      (61.2%), further confirming that mixing all perturbation data doesn't significantly\n      hurt — the correction signal is largely perturbation-agnostic.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-type evaluation matrix (% success rate, 5-seed mean ± std)\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Expert / Eval Type\", \"Visual\", \"Spatial\", \"Physical\", \"Overall\"]\n          - [\"LoRA-V (Visual)\", \"72.0 ± 2.4\", \"57.0 ± 3.0\", \"52.0 ± 2.8\", \"66.0 ± 2.0\"]\n          - [\"LoRA-S (Spatial)\", \"65.0 ± 2.6\", \"64.0 ± 2.8\", \"54.0 ± 2.6\", \"62.9 ± 2.2\"]\n          - [\"LoRA-P (Physical)\", \"63.0 ± 2.8\", \"55.0 ± 3.2\", \"61.0 ± 3.0\", \"61.2 ± 2.4\"]\n          - [\"Universal r16\", \"60.6 ± 2.2\", \"57.0 ± 2.6\", \"55.8 ± 2.4\", \"59.2 ± 2.0\"]\n          - [\"Frozen pi0\", \"51.6 ± 1.8\", \"59.0 ± 2.0\", \"56.4 ± 2.2\", \"53.6 ± 1.8\"]\n      - title: \"Cross-type mismatch drop (pp, matched − avg mismatched)\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Perturbation Type\", \"Matched Expert\", \"Avg Mismatched\", \"Drop (pp)\"]\n          - [\"Visual\", \"72.0 (LoRA-V)\", \"64.0\", \"8.0\"]\n          - [\"Spatial\", \"64.0 (LoRA-S)\", \"56.0\", \"8.0\"]\n          - [\"Physical\", \"61.0 (LoRA-P)\", \"53.0\", \"8.0\"]\n          - [\"Average\", \"—\", \"—\", \"8.0\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Heatmap of 3×3 cross-type matrix showing expert performance on matched vs mismatched types. Diagonal cells (matched) are only mildly warmer than off-diagonal, visually confirming interchangeability.\"\n      - \"Training loss curves for 4 experts showing convergence. LoRA-V converges fastest (epoch 8), LoRA-P slowest (epoch 16) due to limited training data.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 8pp average cross-type drop is at the upper bound of interchangeability but\n      critically insufficient for routing to provide large system-level gains. Each expert\n      learns a general robustness correction that transfers across types — LoRA-V trained on\n      visual augmentation also improves spatial performance to 57% (vs frozen 59%, only\n      2pp loss), and LoRA-S trained on spatial oracle demos achieves 65% on visual\n      (vs LoRA-V's 72%, only 7pp gap). The flow-matching objective drives all experts\n      toward the same dominant correction subspace in action space. Expert training data\n      quality varies significantly: visual augmentation is cheap and abundant (1200 demos),\n      while spatial and physical oracle data is expensive and limited (185-210 demos),\n      partially explaining the performance asymmetry. Universal LoRA r16 (59.2%) outperforms\n      the weakest cross-type entries (LoRA-V on physical: 52.0%), suggesting that mixed\n      training provides better regularization than type-mismatched application.\n\n  - name: \"Rank-48 parameter-matched universal LoRA — existential control\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      Train universal LoRA with rank 48 (~12M params matching Meta-Brain total budget)\n      on mixed all-perturbation data. The #1 critical experiment: does routing add value\n      beyond raw parameter scaling?\n    expected_results:\n      rank48_overall: 62.4\n      rank48_visual: 64.6\n      rank48_spatial: 59.8\n      rank48_physical: 56.0\n      rank48_visual_camera: 57.0\n      rank48_visual_light: 66.0\n      rank48_visual_background: 69.4\n      rank48_visual_noise: 66.0\n      rank48_vs_meta_brain_gap: -1.4\n      rank48_vs_rank16_gain: 3.2\n      rank48_val_mse: 0.0129\n      rank48_train_epochs: 10\n      rank48_convergence_stable: true\n      claim2_survival: FAIL\n      std_5seed: 2.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Rank-48 universal LoRA reaches 62.4% — only 1.4pp below Meta-Brain (63.8%). This gap\n      is not statistically significant with 5 seeds (p=0.18, paired t-test). The existential\n      control FAILS: routing adds no meaningful value over capacity scaling. The rank-48\n      model has ~12M trainable parameters (matching Meta-Brain's 3 × rank-16 experts), and\n      simply scaling LoRA rank with mixed training data captures most of the correction\n      capacity. This occurs because the flow-matching correction signal is fundamentally\n      low-rank and perturbation-agnostic — a rank-48 universal adapter has sufficient\n      capacity to learn the dominant correction directions for ALL perturbation types\n      simultaneously. The rank-48 model is also strictly simpler (no router, no expert\n      selection) and achieves comparable results, making Meta-Brain's routing overhead\n      unjustifiable. The 3.2pp gain over rank-16 (59.2% → 62.4%) confirms that parameter\n      scaling helps monotonically, while the routing approach (rank-16 × 3 experts, 63.8%)\n      only marginally exceeds the capacity-equivalent uniform baseline.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Parameter-matched comparison (5-seed mean ± std)\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Method\", \"Params\", \"Visual\", \"Spatial\", \"Physical\", \"Overall\", \"p vs MB\"]\n          - [\"Universal r16\", \"3.7M\", \"60.6 ± 2.2\", \"57.0 ± 2.6\", \"55.8 ± 2.4\", \"59.2 ± 2.0\", \"0.003\"]\n          - [\"Universal r48\", \"~12M\", \"64.6 ± 2.0\", \"59.8 ± 2.4\", \"56.0 ± 2.6\", \"62.4 ± 2.0\", \"0.18\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"~12M\", \"67.1 ± 2.2\", \"59.2 ± 2.6\", \"55.2 ± 2.8\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: rank-16 vs rank-48 vs Meta-Brain overall success rate with error bars. The rank-48 and Meta-Brain bars overlap within confidence intervals, visually killing the routing advantage claim.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Claim 2 collapses. The 1.4pp gap between Meta-Brain and rank-48 universal is within\n      the noise floor of 5-seed evaluation (±2.0pp std). A reviewer would correctly conclude\n      that the ~12M parameter budget is the primary driver of performance, not the routing\n      architecture. The rank-48 model's per-type breakdown reveals it performs most uniformly\n      across types (V=64.6, S=59.8, P=56.0, spread=8.6pp) compared to Meta-Brain\n      (V=67.1, S=59.2, P=55.2, spread=11.9pp), suggesting the universal model actually\n      provides MORE balanced robustness. Meta-Brain's advantage is concentrated entirely on\n      visual perturbations (+2.5pp) at the cost of slightly worse spatial (-0.6pp) and\n      physical (-0.8pp) performance — a dubious tradeoff.\n\n  - name: \"Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6) + 3 LoRA experts with soft routing.\n      End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_overall: 63.8\n      meta_brain_visual: 67.1\n      meta_brain_spatial: 59.2\n      meta_brain_physical: 55.2\n      meta_brain_visual_camera: 60.0\n      meta_brain_visual_light: 69.0\n      meta_brain_visual_background: 72.4\n      meta_brain_visual_noise: 67.0\n      meta_brain_spatial_layout: 59.2\n      meta_brain_physical_robot: 55.2\n      routing_weight_visual_on_visual: 0.40\n      routing_weight_spatial_on_visual: 0.32\n      routing_weight_physical_on_visual: 0.28\n      routing_weight_spatial_on_spatial: 0.41\n      routing_weight_visual_on_spatial: 0.33\n      routing_weight_physical_on_spatial: 0.26\n      routing_weight_physical_on_physical: 0.39\n      routing_weight_visual_on_physical: 0.34\n      routing_weight_spatial_on_physical: 0.27\n      routing_finetune_epochs: 12\n      routing_finetune_val_mse: 0.0136\n      par_inference_accuracy: 0.994\n      std_5seed: 2.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      The critical finding is routing weight collapse: end-to-end fine-tuning converges to\n      near-uniform routing weights (~0.40/0.32/0.28 for matched/second/third) despite PAR\n      achieving 99.4% classification accuracy. The routing fine-tuning loss landscape is\n      nearly flat with respect to routing sharpness because experts are interchangeable —\n      routing any combination of experts yields similar action MSE. The optimizer settles at\n      a broad minimum where all experts contribute roughly equally, effectively averaging\n      their corrections. This explains why Meta-Brain (63.8%) barely outperforms random\n      routing (63.5%): the learned routing IS approximately random. The routing weights show\n      a mild diagonal pattern (matched type gets 39-41% weight vs expected 33.3% for uniform),\n      but this 7-8pp excess weight on the matched expert translates to <0.5pp downstream\n      benefit because the correction difference between matched and mismatched experts is\n      only 8pp (cross-type drop). The system-level performance (63.8%) is actually LOWER than\n      the standalone matched expert performance for visual (72.0%) because soft routing\n      dilutes the LoRA-V correction with 60% weight from LoRA-S and LoRA-P corrections,\n      which add noise to the visual correction signal.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Learned soft routing weight distribution (avg over 5 seeds)\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Perturbation Type\", \"p(Visual)\", \"p(Spatial)\", \"p(Physical)\", \"Entropy\"]\n          - [\"Visual episodes\", \"0.40\", \"0.32\", \"0.28\", \"1.57\"]\n          - [\"Spatial episodes\", \"0.33\", \"0.41\", \"0.26\", \"1.56\"]\n          - [\"Physical episodes\", \"0.34\", \"0.27\", \"0.39\", \"1.57\"]\n          - [\"Uniform baseline\", \"0.33\", \"0.33\", \"0.33\", \"1.58\"]\n      - title: \"LIBERO-Plus per-perturbation breakdown\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Perturbation\", \"Meta-Brain\", \"Frozen pi0\", \"Gain\"]\n          - [\"Camera\", \"60.0 ± 3.0\", \"44.0 ± 2.6\", \"+16.0\"]\n          - [\"Light\", \"69.0 ± 2.4\", \"53.0 ± 2.2\", \"+16.0\"]\n          - [\"Background\", \"72.4 ± 2.2\", \"57.4 ± 2.0\", \"+15.0\"]\n          - [\"Noise\", \"67.0 ± 2.6\", \"53.0 ± 2.4\", \"+14.0\"]\n          - [\"Layout\", \"59.2 ± 3.2\", \"59.0 ± 2.0\", \"+0.2\"]\n          - [\"Robot\", \"55.2 ± 3.0\", \"56.4 ± 2.2\", \"-1.2\"]\n          - [\"Overall\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"53.6 ± 1.8\", \"+10.2\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Routing weight heatmap: 3×3 matrix showing p(expert|perturbation_type). Should be near-diagonal if routing is effective; instead shows near-uniform pattern with barely visible diagonal tendency.\"\n      - \"Per-perturbation bar chart comparing Meta-Brain vs frozen. Visual perturbations show strong gains (+14-16pp), but spatial (+0.2pp) and physical (-1.2pp) show negligible or negative correction.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The near-uniform routing weights are the smoking gun of Claim 2's failure. PAR correctly\n      CLASSIFIES perturbation types (99.4% accuracy) but the downstream routing fine-tuning\n      cannot translate this into sharp expert selection because the experts' corrections are\n      not sufficiently differentiated. The routing weight entropy (1.57 nats) is within 0.01\n      of maximum entropy (1.58 nats for uniform), meaning the router learns almost zero\n      useful routing signal. Notably, the per-perturbation breakdown reveals an asymmetry:\n      visual perturbations benefit strongly (+14-16pp over frozen) while spatial (+0.2pp)\n      and physical (-1.2pp) show negligible or negative gains. This suggests the LoRA bank\n      predominantly learns visual corrections (the largest training data category) regardless\n      of routing. Physical perturbations show slight DEGRADATION — the LoRA corrections\n      actively interfere with pi0's already-reasonable physical handling (frozen achieves\n      56.4% on robot perturbations). The overall +10.2pp gain over frozen is driven entirely\n      by visual improvements, raising the question of whether a visual-only solution (like\n      FTM) would be more parameter-efficient.\n\n  - name: \"Oracle routing ablation — upper bound for routing quality\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels for hard one-hot routing.\n      Measures the ceiling for routing-based correction with current experts.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_overall: 68.8\n      oracle_visual: 72.0\n      oracle_spatial: 64.0\n      oracle_physical: 61.0\n      oracle_minus_meta_brain: 5.0\n      oracle_minus_random: 5.3\n      oracle_visual_camera: 64.0\n      oracle_visual_light: 74.0\n      oracle_visual_background: 77.0\n      oracle_visual_noise: 73.0\n      std_5seed: 1.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      Oracle routing (68.8%) reveals a 5.0pp gap over Meta-Brain (63.8%), demonstrating that\n      hard one-hot routing to the correctly matched expert significantly outperforms soft\n      blended routing. This gap arises NOT from routing accuracy (PAR is 99.4% accurate)\n      but from the soft-vs-hard routing distinction: Meta-Brain applies diluted corrections\n      (matched expert receives only 40% weight due to routing weight collapse), while oracle\n      applies the full matched expert correction at 100% weight. The oracle result matches\n      the standalone expert-on-own-type performance from plan_002 (LoRA-V=72.0 on visual,\n      LoRA-S=64.0 on spatial, LoRA-P=61.0 on physical), confirming that hard routing\n      eliminates the inter-expert interference that plagues soft routing. The oracle gap\n      serves as evidence that routing COULD provide meaningful benefit if the system used\n      hard routing with high-confidence predictions — but this requires solving the routing\n      weight collapse problem, which is fundamentally tied to expert interchangeability.\n      If experts produced qualitatively different corrections, the routing fine-tuning loss\n      would have sharper gradients favoring correct expert selection.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Oracle vs Meta-Brain per-type comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Type\", \"Oracle\", \"Meta-Brain\", \"Gap\", \"Explanation\"]\n          - [\"Visual\", \"72.0\", \"67.1\", \"+4.9\", \"Soft routing dilutes LoRA-V with 60% wrong-expert weight\"]\n          - [\"Spatial\", \"64.0\", \"59.2\", \"+4.8\", \"LoRA-S correction diluted by 59% non-spatial weight\"]\n          - [\"Physical\", \"61.0\", \"55.2\", \"+5.8\", \"Largest gap — LoRA-P has 61% non-physical weight and physical corrections are most sensitive to interference\"]\n          - [\"Overall\", \"68.8\", \"63.8\", \"+5.0\", \"Consistent gap driven by soft routing dilution\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Ablation bar chart: Frozen → Universal r16 → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle, showing the oracle jump as the largest single step in the ladder.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 5.0pp oracle gap is the one lifeline for the routing paradigm: it proves that\n      if the routing mechanism were improved (hard routing with correct selection), performance\n      would meaningfully increase. However, this lifeline is tenuous: (1) the gap comes from\n      soft-vs-hard routing, not routing accuracy, so simply improving PAR won't help;\n      (2) hard routing with PAR's 99.4% accuracy would nearly match oracle, but would\n      require a binary routing decision that current soft-routing architecture doesn't support;\n      (3) the oracle upper bound (68.8%) still trails the literature's best LIBERO-Plus\n      results from full-model training approaches. The oracle gap is best framed as an open\n      problem: \"How to train LoRA experts that are sufficiently specialized for hard routing\n      to outperform capacity-matched uniform adaptation?\"\n\n  - name: \"Ablation ladder — Frozen → Universal LoRA → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Systematic 5-method comparison on LIBERO-Plus with 5 seeds. Core evidence table for\n      the paper, including the existential rank-48 control.\n    expected_results:\n      frozen_overall: 53.6\n      universal_r16_overall: 59.2\n      universal_r48_overall: 62.4\n      random_routing_overall: 63.5\n      meta_brain_overall: 63.8\n      oracle_overall: 68.8\n      gain_frozen_to_r16: 5.6\n      gain_r16_to_r48: 3.2\n      gain_r48_to_random: 1.1\n      gain_random_to_meta_brain: 0.3\n      gain_meta_brain_to_oracle: 5.0\n      meta_brain_minus_rank48_p_value: 0.18\n      meta_brain_minus_random_p_value: 0.42\n      frozen_visual: 51.6\n      frozen_spatial: 59.0\n      frozen_physical: 56.4\n      r16_visual: 60.6\n      r16_spatial: 57.0\n      r16_physical: 55.8\n      r48_visual: 64.6\n      r48_spatial: 59.8\n      r48_physical: 56.0\n      random_visual: 66.7\n      random_spatial: 58.7\n      random_physical: 55.7\n      meta_brain_visual: 67.1\n      meta_brain_spatial: 59.2\n      meta_brain_physical: 55.2\n      oracle_visual: 72.0\n      oracle_spatial: 64.0\n      oracle_physical: 61.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      The ablation ladder reveals the core worst-case finding: performance scaling is driven\n      by parameter count, not routing architecture. The gains decompose as: Frozen→r16\n      (+5.6pp, adding 3.7M trainable params), r16→r48 (+3.2pp, adding 8.4M params), r48→\n      Meta-Brain (+1.4pp, same param budget but with routing — NOT significant at p=0.18),\n      Meta-Brain→Oracle (+5.0pp, perfect routing with hard selection). The two critical\n      comparisons both fail: (1) Meta-Brain vs rank-48 universal: gap=1.4pp, p=0.18, routing\n      adds no value beyond capacity scaling; (2) Meta-Brain vs random routing: gap=0.3pp,\n      p=0.42, routing is indistinguishable from random expert assignment. The random routing\n      baseline (63.5%) is the most damaging data point — it proves that the multi-expert\n      advantage comes from the ENSEMBLE effect (3 diverse LoRA corrections averaged) not from\n      intelligent routing. Any combination of 3 diverse LoRA experts gives similar results\n      regardless of which expert is selected for which perturbation type. The step from\n      rank-48 to random routing (+1.1pp) shows a small ensemble benefit, and the step from\n      random to Meta-Brain (+0.3pp) shows routing adds essentially nothing on top.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Complete ablation ladder (LIBERO-Plus, 5-seed mean ± std)\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Method\", \"Params\", \"Camera\", \"Light\", \"BG\", \"Noise\", \"Layout\", \"Robot\", \"Overall\"]\n          - [\"Frozen pi0\", \"0\", \"44.0±2.6\", \"53.0±2.2\", \"57.4±2.0\", \"53.0±2.4\", \"59.0±2.0\", \"56.4±2.2\", \"53.6±1.8\"]\n          - [\"Univ r16\", \"3.7M\", \"53.4±2.8\", \"62.0±2.4\", \"65.0±2.2\", \"62.0±2.6\", \"57.0±2.6\", \"55.8±2.4\", \"59.2±2.0\"]\n          - [\"Univ r48\", \"~12M\", \"57.0±2.4\", \"66.0±2.2\", \"69.4±2.0\", \"66.0±2.4\", \"59.8±2.4\", \"56.0±2.6\", \"62.4±2.0\"]\n          - [\"Random\", \"~12M\", \"59.0±3.0\", \"69.0±2.6\", \"72.0±2.2\", \"66.8±2.8\", \"58.7±3.0\", \"55.7±2.8\", \"63.5±2.4\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"~12M\", \"60.0±3.0\", \"69.0±2.4\", \"72.4±2.2\", \"67.0±2.6\", \"59.2±3.2\", \"55.2±3.0\", \"63.8±2.1\"]\n          - [\"Oracle\", \"~12M\", \"64.0±2.0\", \"74.0±1.8\", \"77.0±1.6\", \"73.0±2.0\", \"64.0±2.2\", \"61.0±2.4\", \"68.8±1.6\"]\n      - title: \"Pairwise statistical tests (paired t-test, 5 seeds)\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Comparison\", \"Gap (pp)\", \"p-value\", \"Significant?\"]\n          - [\"MB vs Rank-48\", \"+1.4\", \"0.18\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"MB vs Random\", \"+0.3\", \"0.42\", \"No\"]\n          - [\"Oracle vs MB\", \"+5.0\", \"0.002\", \"Yes\"]\n          - [\"MB vs Rank-16\", \"+4.6\", \"0.004\", \"Yes\"]\n          - [\"MB vs Frozen\", \"+10.2\", \"< 0.001\", \"Yes\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Stacked ablation bar chart with error bars: Frozen(53.6) → r16(59.2) → r48(62.4) → Random(63.5) → Meta-Brain(63.8) → Oracle(68.8). The r48/Random/Meta-Brain cluster is visually indistinguishable, with oracle as a separate jump.\"\n      - \"Per-perturbation-type grouped bar chart (6 types × 6 methods) showing Meta-Brain advantage concentrates in visual sub-types and vanishes for layout/robot.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ablation ladder tells a clear story: parameter scaling explains the performance\n      trajectory, not routing. The diminishing returns pattern (Frozen→r16: +5.6pp/3.7M =\n      1.51pp/M; r16→r48: +3.2pp/8.4M = 0.38pp/M; r48→MB: +1.4pp/0M routing overhead)\n      shows the value of additional correction capacity tapering off. The critical failure\n      is that the r48→Random→Meta-Brain segment (spanning 1.4pp total) cannot be attributed\n      to routing: random routing captures 79% of the r48-to-oracle headroom (1.1/6.4pp),\n      and Meta-Brain captures only an additional 2% (0.3/6.4pp). The remaining 78%\n      (5.0/6.4pp, the oracle gap) requires hard routing that the current soft architecture\n      cannot deliver. Per-perturbation analysis reveals that Meta-Brain's marginal gains\n      are concentrated entirely in visual types (camera +1pp, background +0.4pp over random)\n      with spatial and physical within noise. This vulnerability to \"visual-only benefit\"\n      critically undermines the paper's core pitch of perturbation-TYPE-SPECIFIC routing.\n\n  - name: \"FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      Reproduce FTM (Fine-Tuned visual robustness Module) on pi0 backbone for visual\n      perturbation types. Compare with Meta-Brain and LoRA-V.\n    expected_results:\n      ftm_visual: 65.0\n      ftm_spatial: 57.4\n      ftm_physical: 55.2\n      ftm_overall: 62.0\n      ftm_params: 4700\n      ftm_camera: 58.0\n      ftm_light: 67.0\n      ftm_background: 70.0\n      ftm_noise: 65.0\n      lora_v_visual: 72.0\n      meta_brain_visual: 67.1\n      meta_brain_overall: 63.8\n      ftm_vs_meta_brain_overall_gap: -1.8\n      ftm_vs_meta_brain_visual_gap: -2.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      FTM reproduced on pi0 achieves 65.0% on visual perturbations with only 4.7K trainable\n      parameters — 89% of Meta-Brain's visual performance (67.1%) with 0.04% of the\n      parameters. FTM's overall performance (62.0%) is only 1.8pp below Meta-Brain (63.8%),\n      a devastating comparison given the 2500× parameter ratio. This occurs because\n      LIBERO-Plus weighs visual perturbations 4:1:1 (4 visual sub-types vs 1 spatial,\n      1 physical), so a method that handles visual well automatically scores high overall.\n      FTM cannot handle spatial/physical perturbations (57.4% and 55.2%, near frozen\n      baseline), but the visual dominance in the benchmark compensates. The reproduction\n      does not fully match FTM's original reported numbers (90%+ on their visual benchmark)\n      because: (1) pi0's PaliGemma backbone differs from FTM's original architecture,\n      (2) LIBERO-Plus visual perturbations are more diverse than FTM's original evaluation\n      set, (3) FTM's 4.7K parameter budget is extremely tight on pi0's 2B-parameter scale.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"FTM vs Meta-Brain visual comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Method\", \"Params\", \"Camera\", \"Light\", \"BG\", \"Noise\", \"Visual Avg\", \"Overall\"]\n          - [\"FTM (pi0)\", \"4.7K\", \"58.0\", \"67.0\", \"70.0\", \"65.0\", \"65.0\", \"62.0\"]\n          - [\"LoRA-V\", \"3.7M\", \"64.0\", \"74.0\", \"77.0\", \"73.0\", \"72.0\", \"66.0\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"~12M\", \"60.0\", \"69.0\", \"72.4\", \"67.0\", \"67.1\", \"63.8\"]\n          - [\"Frozen pi0\", \"0\", \"44.0\", \"53.0\", \"57.4\", \"53.0\", \"51.6\", \"53.6\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Visual-only bar chart: FTM vs LoRA-V vs Meta-Brain on 4 visual sub-types, showing FTM competitive at 2500× fewer parameters.\"\n    analysis: >\n      FTM is a brutal comparison. With 4.7K parameters it achieves 78% of Meta-Brain's\n      gain over frozen on visual perturbations (13.4pp vs 15.5pp gain). For reviewers\n      prioritizing parameter efficiency, FTM + frozen pi0 (62.0% overall) is a strictly\n      better baseline than Meta-Brain (63.8%) when normalized by parameter cost. This\n      reinforces the worst-case narrative that Meta-Brain's routing architecture does not\n      provide sufficient added value: a simple visual augmentation technique captures most\n      of the achievable robustness gain on the benchmark. The meta-lesson is that LIBERO-Plus\n      is structurally biased toward visual perturbation solutions due to its 4:1:1 type\n      weighting, which any visual-specialized method can exploit.\n\n  - name: \"Parameter efficiency analysis — gain-over-baseline vs trainable params\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      Compute gain-over-frozen-baseline normalized by trainable parameters. Produce parameter\n      efficiency scatter plot for all methods.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_gain_over_frozen: 10.2\n      meta_brain_params_M: 12.0\n      meta_brain_gain_per_M: 0.85\n      rank48_gain_over_frozen: 8.8\n      rank48_gain_per_M: 0.73\n      rank16_gain_over_frozen: 5.6\n      rank16_gain_per_M: 1.51\n      ftm_gain_over_frozen_visual_only: 13.4\n      ftm_gain_per_M_visual: 2851.0\n      ftm_gain_over_frozen_overall: 8.4\n      ftm_gain_per_M_overall: 1787.0\n      vla_gse_gain_over_lora: 12.0\n      vla_gse_params_M: 114.0\n      vla_gse_gain_per_M: 0.105\n    result_rationale: >\n      The parameter efficiency analysis reveals Meta-Brain (0.85 pp/M) is only marginally\n      more efficient than rank-48 universal (0.73 pp/M), a 16% improvement in efficiency\n      that does not justify the added routing complexity. Meanwhile, rank-16 universal\n      (1.51 pp/M) is 78% more efficient than Meta-Brain per parameter, suggesting smaller\n      universal adapters are the most efficient approach. FTM dominates the entire plot\n      at 1787 pp/M (overall) or 2851 pp/M (visual-only), orders of magnitude above any\n      LoRA-based approach. The VLA-GSE comparison (0.105 pp/M) is indirect (different\n      backbone) but shows Meta-Brain is ~8× more parameter-efficient — however, this\n      comparison is methodologically weak and a reviewer would dismiss it as apples-to-oranges.\n      The scatter plot will show a clear Pareto front: FTM (top-left, minimal params, good\n      gain), rank-16 (middle), rank-48 ≈ Meta-Brain (overlapping, invalidating routing\n      value), VLA-GSE (bottom-right, most params).\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Parameter efficiency comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Method\", \"Params\", \"Overall Gain (pp)\", \"Gain/M params\", \"Notes\"]\n          - [\"FTM\", \"4.7K\", \"+8.4\", \"1787\", \"Visual-only, 4:1:1 we"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14598,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14598},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd55bb73-8a31-4b84-9f66-dba77b234ae1","uuid":"4c8b3ad6-208c-4e87-aece-ad46725e780b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LhjojrqAKgQGqdEAjumHdL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to continue the YAML output from where it was cut off. I was in the middle of the parameter efficiency table in plan_007. Let me continue from there and complete all remaining experiments. I'll output the rest of the YAML directly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":63894,"cache_read_input_tokens":26045,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":63894},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd55bb73-8a31-4b84-9f66-dba77b234ae1","uuid":"719887e4-3972-4c87-91b5-48cafbaa3cf0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LhjojrqAKgQGqdEAjumHdL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\nighting bias\"]\n          - [\"Universal r16\", \"3.7M\", \"+5.6\", \"1.51\", \"Most efficient LoRA\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"~12M\", \"+10.2\", \"0.85\", \"Routing overhead not justified\"]\n          - [\"Universal r48\", \"~12M\", \"+8.8\", \"0.73\", \"Capacity-matched, no routing\"]\n          - [\"VLA-GSE\", \"114M\", \"+12.0\", \"0.105\", \"Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Log-scale scatter plot: x=trainable params, y=gain-over-frozen. FTM at top-left corner, rank-16 in sweet spot, rank-48 and Meta-Brain overlapping in middle, VLA-GSE at bottom-right. Pareto front runs through FTM → rank-16, skipping Meta-Brain entirely.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The scatter plot is the visual death of Claim 3. Meta-Brain does NOT lie on the\n      Pareto front — rank-16 universal achieves better gain-per-parameter, and rank-48\n      universal achieves comparable absolute gain at the same parameter budget. The only\n      way to rescue the efficiency story is the VLA-GSE comparison (8× more efficient),\n      but this is cross-backbone and a reviewer will dismiss it. FTM's dominance at 4.7K\n      params exposes the benchmark's visual bias and makes any LoRA-based approach look\n      over-engineered for the dominant perturbation category.\n\n  - name: \"Correction Memory ablation — with/without CM\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM adds routing-weight\n      modulation from FAISS-retrieved exemplars.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_no_cm: 63.8\n      meta_brain_with_cm: 64.6\n      cm_delta_overall: 0.8\n      cm_delta_visual: 1.0\n      cm_delta_spatial: 0.8\n      cm_delta_physical: 0.4\n      cm_alpha_learned: 0.12\n      cm_retrieval_latency_ms: 2.4\n      cm_p_value_vs_no_cm: 0.31\n      std_5seed_cm: 2.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      CM adds +0.8pp overall (p=0.31, not significant). The learned modulation coefficient\n      alpha converges to 0.12, meaning CM barely adjusts routing weights — it scales\n      exemplar-derived routing corrections by only 12% before applying them. This confirms\n      dream reviewer consensus that CM is padding: the FAISS retrieval correctly identifies\n      similar past episodes, but the retrieved routing weight adjustments are near-zero\n      because the base routing weights are already near-uniform. CM cannot rescue routing\n      weight collapse — it modulates weights that are already effectively random. The\n      visual-type benefit (+1.0pp) is largest because visual perturbations have the most\n      training exemplars in the FAISS index, giving higher retrieval quality. Physical\n      benefit (+0.4pp) is negligible due to sparse exemplar coverage.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"CM ablation (5-seed mean ± std)\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Method\", \"Visual\", \"Spatial\", \"Physical\", \"Overall\", \"p vs no-CM\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"67.1 ± 2.2\", \"59.2 ± 2.6\", \"55.2 ± 2.8\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain + CM\", \"68.1 ± 2.0\", \"60.0 ± 2.4\", \"55.6 ± 2.8\", \"64.6 ± 2.0\", \"0.31\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Minimal delta bar chart showing CM contribution per type — all bars within error range.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CM should be relegated to a single-sentence footnote in the ablation table. The +0.8pp\n      gain does not justify the added complexity (FAISS index, retrieval pipeline, alpha\n      parameter). Dream reviewers correctly identified this as padding — do not feature CM\n      in the architecture diagram or contribution list.\n\n  - name: \"Router architecture comparison — MLP vs linear vs attention\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Compare PAR router variants on PaliGemma layer 6: 2-layer MLP, linear classifier,\n      attention-based router. Test if routing accuracy differences translate downstream.\n    expected_results:\n      mlp_router_f1: 0.996\n      mlp_downstream_overall: 63.8\n      linear_router_f1: 0.982\n      linear_downstream_overall: 63.4\n      attention_router_f1: 0.994\n      attention_downstream_overall: 63.6\n      max_downstream_spread: 0.4\n      mlp_params: 526000\n      linear_params: 6150\n      attention_params: 1050000\n    result_rationale: >\n      Router architecture has negligible downstream impact (0.4pp spread across all\n      variants). The linear classifier achieves 98.2% F1 with 6.1K parameters and yields\n      63.4% downstream — only 0.4pp below the 2-layer MLP (99.6% F1, 526K params). This\n      confirms that routing accuracy beyond ~98% does not translate to downstream gains\n      because the bottleneck is expert interchangeability, not routing quality. Even a\n      1.4pp F1 improvement (linear→MLP) yields only 0.4pp downstream — a 3.5× attenuation\n      ratio. The attention-based router adds parameters (1.05M) without benefit. This\n      experiment supports the worst-case thesis: investing in better routers is futile when\n      experts don't differentiate.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Router architecture comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Router\", \"Params\", \"Classification F1\", \"Downstream Overall\", \"Δ vs MLP\"]\n          - [\"Linear\", \"6.1K\", \"0.982\", \"63.4 ± 2.2\", \"-0.4\"]\n          - [\"MLP (2-layer)\", \"526K\", \"0.996\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Attention\", \"1.05M\", \"0.994\", \"63.6 ± 2.2\", \"-0.2\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter: x=router classification F1, y=downstream success rate. Nearly flat line showing classification accuracy does not predict downstream performance.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Use the linear router result (6.1K params, 63.4%) to argue that even a minimal router\n      captures all available routing benefit. The flat accuracy-vs-downstream curve is\n      strong evidence that routing quality is not the bottleneck. Report as a single row\n      in the ablation table, not a standalone figure.\n\n  - name: \"6-class vs 3-class routing comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot)\n      with 6 LoRA experts vs 3-class coarse routing with 3 experts.\n    expected_results:\n      three_class_overall: 63.8\n      six_class_overall: 62.0\n      three_class_params: 12000000\n      six_class_params: 12000000\n      six_class_per_expert_rank: 8\n      three_class_per_expert_rank: 16\n      six_class_visual_camera: 59.0\n      six_class_visual_light: 66.0\n      six_class_visual_bg: 68.0\n      six_class_visual_noise: 63.0\n      six_class_spatial: 58.0\n      six_class_physical: 56.0\n      six_class_router_f1: 0.988\n    result_rationale: >\n      6-class routing (62.0%) actually UNDERPERFORMS 3-class routing (63.8%) by 1.8pp\n      despite identical total parameter budget (~12M). At 6 experts, each expert has rank 8\n      (~2M params) vs rank 16 (~3.7M) for 3-class, and the reduced per-expert capacity\n      more than offsets any benefit from finer-grained routing. Each rank-8 expert has\n      insufficient capacity to learn even the general correction signal that rank-16 experts\n      capture. The 6-class router maintains high accuracy (F1=0.988) — the problem is not\n      classification but per-expert underfitting. Intra-visual sub-types (camera vs light\n      vs background vs noise) are too similar in correction needs to justify separate experts:\n      Phase 0 showed these are closer to each other than to spatial/physical, and the expert\n      training confirms this — splitting visual into 4 sub-experts wastes capacity on\n      redundant specialization.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"3-class vs 6-class routing\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Config\", \"Experts\", \"Rank/expert\", \"Total params\", \"Overall\", \"Δ\"]\n          - [\"3-class\", \"3\", \"16\", \"~12M\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"6-class\", \"6\", \"8\", \"~12M\", \"62.0 ± 2.4\", \"-1.8\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Grouped bar chart: per-perturbation-type comparison of 3-class vs 6-class, showing 6-class consistently lower due to capacity starvation.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Finer-grained routing hurts when expert capacity is constrained. This is consistent\n      with the worst-case thesis: the correction benefit comes from parameter capacity,\n      not routing granularity. Splitting parameters into more experts dilutes capacity\n      without sufficient specialization benefit. Report as a single row in ablation,\n      supporting the conclusion that 3-class taxonomy is already too fine if routing\n      itself doesn't help.\n\n  - name: \"LoRA rank sweep (r=16/32/64) — capacity bottleneck test\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_014\"\n    description: >\n      Test whether routing benefit grows with expert capacity by training 3-expert\n      Meta-Brain at rank 16/32/64 and comparing with parameter-matched universal LoRA.\n    expected_results:\n      r16_meta_brain_overall: 63.8\n      r16_universal_matched_overall: 62.4\n      r16_routing_benefit: 1.4\n      r32_meta_brain_overall: 66.0\n      r32_universal_matched_overall: 64.4\n      r32_routing_benefit: 1.6\n      r64_meta_brain_overall: 68.4\n      r64_universal_matched_overall: 66.2\n      r64_routing_benefit: 2.2\n      r16_total_params_M: 12\n      r32_total_params_M: 24\n      r64_total_params_M: 48\n      routing_benefit_slope_pp_per_doubling: 0.4\n      routing_benefit_significant_at_any_rank: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      Routing benefit grows marginally with rank (1.4pp → 1.6pp → 2.2pp) but remains\n      statistically insignificant at all tested ranks. The slope is ~0.4pp per rank\n      doubling — extrapolating, routing would need rank ~512 (300M+ params) to reach a\n      meaningful 5pp benefit, at which point the parameter budget exceeds the entire\n      backbone and the approach is absurd. At each rank level, the parameter-matched\n      universal LoRA captures 93-97% of Meta-Brain's performance. The slight upward trend\n      suggests experts DO become marginally more specialized at higher capacity (more\n      parameters allow learning type-specific correction subspaces beyond the shared\n      dominant directions), but the rate of specialization growth is far too slow. The\n      fundamental issue persists: flow-matching training drives experts toward the same\n      dominant correction directions regardless of rank, and rank scaling primarily adds\n      capacity for the SHARED correction signal rather than the type-specific residual.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Rank sweep: routing benefit at each capacity level\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Rank\", \"MB Total Params\", \"Meta-Brain\", \"Univ (matched)\", \"Routing Benefit\", \"p-value\"]\n          - [\"16\", \"~12M\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"62.4 ± 2.0\", \"+1.4\", \"0.18\"]\n          - [\"32\", \"~24M\", \"66.0 ± 1.8\", \"64.4 ± 1.8\", \"+1.6\", \"0.14\"]\n          - [\"64\", \"~48M\", \"68.4 ± 1.6\", \"66.2 ± 1.6\", \"+2.2\", \"0.08\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Line plot: x=expert rank (log scale), y=routing benefit (pp). Near-flat line with slight upward trend, all points below the 5pp significance threshold. Extrapolation dashed line shows impractical rank needed for meaningful benefit.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The rank sweep is the definitive nail: routing benefit does not emerge at any tested\n      capacity. The worst-case dream hypothesis predicted no capacity threshold rescue —\n      confirmed. Even at r=64 with 48M total params (4× the original budget), routing adds\n      only 2.2pp (p=0.08). The trend is real but economically irrational: spending 4×\n      the parameter budget for a 0.8pp improvement in routing benefit. Frame in the paper\n      as: \"Routing benefit grows sub-linearly with expert capacity, suggesting that\n      the correction subspace learned by LoRA experts is dominated by a shared\n      perturbation-agnostic component at all tested ranks.\"\n\n  - name: \"Clean LIBERO evaluation — nominal performance preservation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_015\"\n    description: >\n      Evaluate Meta-Brain and baselines on standard (unperturbed) LIBERO to verify routing\n      does not degrade nominal performance.\n    expected_results:\n      frozen_clean: 92.0\n      universal_r16_clean: 90.4\n      universal_r48_clean: 89.6\n      random_routing_clean: 88.8\n      meta_brain_clean: 87.8\n      oracle_clean: 88.2\n      meta_brain_clean_delta: -4.2\n      r48_clean_delta: -2.4\n      r16_clean_delta: -1.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      Meta-Brain degrades clean LIBERO by -4.2pp (92.0% → 87.8%), the largest degradation\n      among all methods. This occurs because the routing mechanism CANNOT detect \"no\n      perturbation\" as a distinct state — PAR was trained on 3-class perturbation classification\n      and always routes to some expert. On clean episodes, LoRA corrections are actively\n      harmful: they adjust representations that were already correct, introducing noise into\n      the action predictions. The degradation hierarchy (r16: -1.6pp, r48: -2.4pp,\n      Meta-Brain: -4.2pp) correlates with intervention magnitude — more LoRA correction =\n      more damage on clean inputs. Oracle routing (88.2%) also degrades because it still\n      applies expert corrections (just the \"correct\" one for the predicted type), and no\n      expert is correct for clean inputs. Notably, random routing (88.8%) degrades LESS\n      than Meta-Brain (87.8%), suggesting that Meta-Brain's near-uniform soft routing\n      distributes correction noise more uniformly across layers than random hard routing's\n      single-expert corrections. The -4.2pp clean degradation is a significant practical\n      concern: a deployment that encounters a mix of perturbed and clean episodes would\n      see Meta-Brain's net benefit diminished. If 50% of deployment episodes are clean,\n      the net effect is (+10.2 × 0.5) + (-4.2 × 0.5) = +3.0pp — barely better than\n      rank-16 universal's +5.6pp gain with negligible clean degradation.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Clean LIBERO performance\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Method\", \"Clean LIBERO (%)\", \"Δ vs Frozen\", \"LIBERO-Plus (%)\", \"Net (50/50 mix)\"]\n          - [\"Frozen pi0\", \"92.0\", \"—\", \"53.6\", \"72.8\"]\n          - [\"Universal r16\", \"90.4\", \"-1.6\", \"59.2\", \"74.8\"]\n          - [\"Universal r48\", \"89.6\", \"-2.4\", \"62.4\", \"76.0\"]\n          - [\"Random routing\", \"88.8\", \"-3.2\", \"63.5\", \"76.2\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"87.8\", \"-4.2\", \"63.8\", \"75.8\"]\n          - [\"Oracle\", \"88.2\", \"-3.8\", \"68.8\", \"78.5\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dual-axis bar chart: LIBERO-Plus gain (up) vs Clean LIBERO loss (down) per method, showing Meta-Brain's unfavorable gain/loss ratio compared to simpler baselines.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The clean degradation story is damaging for deployment. The 50/50 mix column reveals\n      that Universal r48 (76.0%) actually outperforms Meta-Brain (75.8%) in realistic\n      mixed scenarios, because r48's smaller clean degradation (-2.4pp vs -4.2pp) offsets\n      its slightly lower LIBERO-Plus performance. A reviewer focused on practical deployment\n      would prefer r48 + its simplicity over Meta-Brain + routing overhead. The paper should\n      acknowledge this limitation honestly and suggest a perturbation DETECTION gate (using\n      Phase 0 binary classifier) to disable LoRA corrections on clean inputs — but this\n      adds yet another module and further undermines the elegance of the routing approach.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-backbone validation — SmolVLA\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Replicate Phase 0 + Phase 1 core pipeline on SmolVLA to test generalizability of\n      perturbation-diagnostic routing across architectures.\n    expected_results:\n      smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.968\n      smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n      smolvla_phase0_k1_f1: 0.91\n      smolvla_frozen_libero_plus: 48.2\n      smolvla_universal_r16_overall: 54.0\n      smolvla_meta_brain_overall: 55.2\n      smolvla_routing_gap_vs_universal: 1.2\n      smolvla_random_routing_overall: 54.8\n      smolvla_routing_gap_vs_random: 0.4\n      smolvla_total_gpu_hours: 28\n    result_rationale: >\n      Phase 0 transfers well to SmolVLA (3-class F1=0.968), confirming the diagnostic\n      finding is architecture-general: VLA internal representations encode perturbation type\n      regardless of backbone. The best classification layer shifts (layer 4 vs pi0's layer 6)\n      due to SmolVLA's different depth, but the single-layer, single-token classification\n      property holds. However, Phase 1 routing shows the same failure pattern: Meta-Brain\n      (55.2%) only marginally outperforms universal r16 (54.0%, gap=1.2pp) and random\n      routing (54.8%, gap=0.4pp). The routing benefit is even smaller than on pi0 (1.2pp\n      vs 1.4pp), consistent with SmolVLA's smaller model having less capacity for expert\n      specialization. This cross-backbone result strengthens the Phase 0 finding (diagnostic\n      signal is universal) while simultaneously confirming the Phase 1 negative result\n      (routing doesn't help) generalizes across architectures.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"SmolVLA cross-backbone summary\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Metric\", \"pi0\", \"SmolVLA\"]\n          - [\"Phase 0 3-class F1\", \"0.993\", \"0.968\"]\n          - [\"Phase 0 best layer K=1\", \"layer 6 (F1=1.0)\", \"layer 4 (F1=0.91)\"]\n          - [\"Frozen baseline\", \"53.6%\", \"48.2%\"]\n          - [\"MB vs Universal r16 gap\", \"+4.6pp\", \"+1.2pp\"]\n          - [\"MB vs Random gap\", \"+0.3pp\", \"+0.4pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side ablation ladders for pi0 and SmolVLA, showing parallel structure: Phase 0 succeeds, Phase 1 routing fails on both.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-backbone validation is the strongest result for the reframed paper. Phase 0\n      generalizes cleanly (0.968 F1 on SmolVLA), while Phase 1 fails consistently — this\n      supports reframing as \"VLA internals encode perturbation type (generalizable finding)\n      but routing-based correction doesn't exploit it (negative result).\" Use SmolVLA data\n      to strengthen Phase 0 as the primary contribution and present routing failure as\n      architecture-independent, not pi0-specific.\n\n  - name: \"LoRA layer placement ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA experts: early (2-6), mid (6-12), late\n      (12-16), all, and matched placement (V→early, S→mid, P→late).\n    expected_results:\n      all_layers_overall: 63.8\n      early_only_overall: 62.2\n      mid_only_overall: 62.8\n      late_only_overall: 61.8\n      matched_placement_overall: 63.2\n      matched_vs_all_gap: -0.6\n      early_visual: 65.0\n      mid_visual: 64.0\n      late_visual: 63.0\n      early_physical: 53.0\n      mid_physical: 55.0\n      late_physical: 56.0\n      matched_visual: 65.4\n      matched_spatial: 60.0\n      matched_physical: 56.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Layer placement shows weak effects (1.6pp spread across configurations). All-layers\n      (63.8%) is best, and matched placement (63.2%) does NOT outperform it, disconfirming\n      the hypothesis that V→early, S→mid, P→late mapping from Phase 0 translates to\n      correction benefit. The matched placement shows mild per-type trends consistent with\n      Phase 0 (early layers slightly better for visual, late for physical), but the effects\n      are within noise. This occurs because: (1) Phase 0's layer mapping describes WHERE\n      perturbation information is encoded, not WHERE corrections should be applied — these\n      are different properties; (2) LoRA corrections propagate through subsequent layers\n      regardless of injection point, so early-layer LoRA eventually affects late-layer\n      representations; (3) the dominant correction signal is distributed across all layers,\n      not localized. The all-layers configuration wins simply by having more parameters\n      applied (72 LoRA modules across 18 layers vs ~24 modules for partial-layer configs),\n      consistent with the capacity-over-specialization theme.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Layer placement ablation\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Config\", \"Layers\", \"LoRA modules\", \"Visual\", \"Spatial\", \"Physical\", \"Overall\"]\n          - [\"Early only\", \"2-6\", \"20\", \"65.0\", \"57.0\", \"53.0\", \"62.2\"]\n          - [\"Mid only\", \"6-12\", \"28\", \"64.0\", \"59.0\", \"55.0\", \"62.8\"]\n          - [\"Late only\", \"12-16\", \"20\", \"63.0\", \"58.0\", \"56.0\", \"61.8\"]\n          - [\"Matched (V→E,S→M,P→L)\", \"mixed\", \"~24\", \"65.4\", \"60.0\", \"56.8\", \"63.2\"]\n          - [\"All layers\", \"2-16\", \"72\", \"67.1\", \"59.2\", \"55.2\", \"63.8\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Layer × perturbation-type heatmap showing mild interaction effects that are within noise of all-layer baseline.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The layer placement ablation provides a nuanced finding for the reframed paper:\n      Phase 0's layer-perturbation mapping (V→early, S→mid, P→late) describes diagnostic\n      encoding, not optimal correction sites. Frame as: \"The mechanistic insight about\n      where perturbation information is encoded does not straightforwardly translate to\n      where corrections should be applied — full-layer coverage remains optimal, suggesting\n      correction requires global representation adjustment rather than targeted layer\n      intervention.\" This is an interesting negative result that adds depth to the\n      mechanistic analysis without overpromising.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":63894,"cache_read_input_tokens":26045,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":63894},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd55bb73-8a31-4b84-9f66-dba77b234ae1","uuid":"299daf6b-2167-4017-9f33-430f2743ef61"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":1260482,"duration_api_ms":1261560,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\nighting bias\"]\n          - [\"Universal r16\", \"3.7M\", \"+5.6\", \"1.51\", \"Most efficient LoRA\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"~12M\", \"+10.2\", \"0.85\", \"Routing overhead not justified\"]\n          - [\"Universal r48\", \"~12M\", \"+8.8\", \"0.73\", \"Capacity-matched, no routing\"]\n          - [\"VLA-GSE\", \"114M\", \"+12.0\", \"0.105\", \"Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Log-scale scatter plot: x=trainable params, y=gain-over-frozen. FTM at top-left corner, rank-16 in sweet spot, rank-48 and Meta-Brain overlapping in middle, VLA-GSE at bottom-right. Pareto front runs through FTM → rank-16, skipping Meta-Brain entirely.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The scatter plot is the visual death of Claim 3. Meta-Brain does NOT lie on the\n      Pareto front — rank-16 universal achieves better gain-per-parameter, and rank-48\n      universal achieves comparable absolute gain at the same parameter budget. The only\n      way to rescue the efficiency story is the VLA-GSE comparison (8× more efficient),\n      but this is cross-backbone and a reviewer will dismiss it. FTM's dominance at 4.7K\n      params exposes the benchmark's visual bias and makes any LoRA-based approach look\n      over-engineered for the dominant perturbation category.\n\n  - name: \"Correction Memory ablation — with/without CM\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM adds routing-weight\n      modulation from FAISS-retrieved exemplars.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_no_cm: 63.8\n      meta_brain_with_cm: 64.6\n      cm_delta_overall: 0.8\n      cm_delta_visual: 1.0\n      cm_delta_spatial: 0.8\n      cm_delta_physical: 0.4\n      cm_alpha_learned: 0.12\n      cm_retrieval_latency_ms: 2.4\n      cm_p_value_vs_no_cm: 0.31\n      std_5seed_cm: 2.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      CM adds +0.8pp overall (p=0.31, not significant). The learned modulation coefficient\n      alpha converges to 0.12, meaning CM barely adjusts routing weights — it scales\n      exemplar-derived routing corrections by only 12% before applying them. This confirms\n      dream reviewer consensus that CM is padding: the FAISS retrieval correctly identifies\n      similar past episodes, but the retrieved routing weight adjustments are near-zero\n      because the base routing weights are already near-uniform. CM cannot rescue routing\n      weight collapse — it modulates weights that are already effectively random. The\n      visual-type benefit (+1.0pp) is largest because visual perturbations have the most\n      training exemplars in the FAISS index, giving higher retrieval quality. Physical\n      benefit (+0.4pp) is negligible due to sparse exemplar coverage.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"CM ablation (5-seed mean ± std)\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Method\", \"Visual\", \"Spatial\", \"Physical\", \"Overall\", \"p vs no-CM\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"67.1 ± 2.2\", \"59.2 ± 2.6\", \"55.2 ± 2.8\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain + CM\", \"68.1 ± 2.0\", \"60.0 ± 2.4\", \"55.6 ± 2.8\", \"64.6 ± 2.0\", \"0.31\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Minimal delta bar chart showing CM contribution per type — all bars within error range.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CM should be relegated to a single-sentence footnote in the ablation table. The +0.8pp\n      gain does not justify the added complexity (FAISS index, retrieval pipeline, alpha\n      parameter). Dream reviewers correctly identified this as padding — do not feature CM\n      in the architecture diagram or contribution list.\n\n  - name: \"Router architecture comparison — MLP vs linear vs attention\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Compare PAR router variants on PaliGemma layer 6: 2-layer MLP, linear classifier,\n      attention-based router. Test if routing accuracy differences translate downstream.\n    expected_results:\n      mlp_router_f1: 0.996\n      mlp_downstream_overall: 63.8\n      linear_router_f1: 0.982\n      linear_downstream_overall: 63.4\n      attention_router_f1: 0.994\n      attention_downstream_overall: 63.6\n      max_downstream_spread: 0.4\n      mlp_params: 526000\n      linear_params: 6150\n      attention_params: 1050000\n    result_rationale: >\n      Router architecture has negligible downstream impact (0.4pp spread across all\n      variants). The linear classifier achieves 98.2% F1 with 6.1K parameters and yields\n      63.4% downstream — only 0.4pp below the 2-layer MLP (99.6% F1, 526K params). This\n      confirms that routing accuracy beyond ~98% does not translate to downstream gains\n      because the bottleneck is expert interchangeability, not routing quality. Even a\n      1.4pp F1 improvement (linear→MLP) yields only 0.4pp downstream — a 3.5× attenuation\n      ratio. The attention-based router adds parameters (1.05M) without benefit. This\n      experiment supports the worst-case thesis: investing in better routers is futile when\n      experts don't differentiate.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Router architecture comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Router\", \"Params\", \"Classification F1\", \"Downstream Overall\", \"Δ vs MLP\"]\n          - [\"Linear\", \"6.1K\", \"0.982\", \"63.4 ± 2.2\", \"-0.4\"]\n          - [\"MLP (2-layer)\", \"526K\", \"0.996\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Attention\", \"1.05M\", \"0.994\", \"63.6 ± 2.2\", \"-0.2\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter: x=router classification F1, y=downstream success rate. Nearly flat line showing classification accuracy does not predict downstream performance.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Use the linear router result (6.1K params, 63.4%) to argue that even a minimal router\n      captures all available routing benefit. The flat accuracy-vs-downstream curve is\n      strong evidence that routing quality is not the bottleneck. Report as a single row\n      in the ablation table, not a standalone figure.\n\n  - name: \"6-class vs 3-class routing comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot)\n      with 6 LoRA experts vs 3-class coarse routing with 3 experts.\n    expected_results:\n      three_class_overall: 63.8\n      six_class_overall: 62.0\n      three_class_params: 12000000\n      six_class_params: 12000000\n      six_class_per_expert_rank: 8\n      three_class_per_expert_rank: 16\n      six_class_visual_camera: 59.0\n      six_class_visual_light: 66.0\n      six_class_visual_bg: 68.0\n      six_class_visual_noise: 63.0\n      six_class_spatial: 58.0\n      six_class_physical: 56.0\n      six_class_router_f1: 0.988\n    result_rationale: >\n      6-class routing (62.0%) actually UNDERPERFORMS 3-class routing (63.8%) by 1.8pp\n      despite identical total parameter budget (~12M). At 6 experts, each expert has rank 8\n      (~2M params) vs rank 16 (~3.7M) for 3-class, and the reduced per-expert capacity\n      more than offsets any benefit from finer-grained routing. Each rank-8 expert has\n      insufficient capacity to learn even the general correction signal that rank-16 experts\n      capture. The 6-class router maintains high accuracy (F1=0.988) — the problem is not\n      classification but per-expert underfitting. Intra-visual sub-types (camera vs light\n      vs background vs noise) are too similar in correction needs to justify separate experts:\n      Phase 0 showed these are closer to each other than to spatial/physical, and the expert\n      training confirms this — splitting visual into 4 sub-experts wastes capacity on\n      redundant specialization.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"3-class vs 6-class routing\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Config\", \"Experts\", \"Rank/expert\", \"Total params\", \"Overall\", \"Δ\"]\n          - [\"3-class\", \"3\", \"16\", \"~12M\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"6-class\", \"6\", \"8\", \"~12M\", \"62.0 ± 2.4\", \"-1.8\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Grouped bar chart: per-perturbation-type comparison of 3-class vs 6-class, showing 6-class consistently lower due to capacity starvation.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Finer-grained routing hurts when expert capacity is constrained. This is consistent\n      with the worst-case thesis: the correction benefit comes from parameter capacity,\n      not routing granularity. Splitting parameters into more experts dilutes capacity\n      without sufficient specialization benefit. Report as a single row in ablation,\n      supporting the conclusion that 3-class taxonomy is already too fine if routing\n      itself doesn't help.\n\n  - name: \"LoRA rank sweep (r=16/32/64) — capacity bottleneck test\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_014\"\n    description: >\n      Test whether routing benefit grows with expert capacity by training 3-expert\n      Meta-Brain at rank 16/32/64 and comparing with parameter-matched universal LoRA.\n    expected_results:\n      r16_meta_brain_overall: 63.8\n      r16_universal_matched_overall: 62.4\n      r16_routing_benefit: 1.4\n      r32_meta_brain_overall: 66.0\n      r32_universal_matched_overall: 64.4\n      r32_routing_benefit: 1.6\n      r64_meta_brain_overall: 68.4\n      r64_universal_matched_overall: 66.2\n      r64_routing_benefit: 2.2\n      r16_total_params_M: 12\n      r32_total_params_M: 24\n      r64_total_params_M: 48\n      routing_benefit_slope_pp_per_doubling: 0.4\n      routing_benefit_significant_at_any_rank: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      Routing benefit grows marginally with rank (1.4pp → 1.6pp → 2.2pp) but remains\n      statistically insignificant at all tested ranks. The slope is ~0.4pp per rank\n      doubling — extrapolating, routing would need rank ~512 (300M+ params) to reach a\n      meaningful 5pp benefit, at which point the parameter budget exceeds the entire\n      backbone and the approach is absurd. At each rank level, the parameter-matched\n      universal LoRA captures 93-97% of Meta-Brain's performance. The slight upward trend\n      suggests experts DO become marginally more specialized at higher capacity (more\n      parameters allow learning type-specific correction subspaces beyond the shared\n      dominant directions), but the rate of specialization growth is far too slow. The\n      fundamental issue persists: flow-matching training drives experts toward the same\n      dominant correction directions regardless of rank, and rank scaling primarily adds\n      capacity for the SHARED correction signal rather than the type-specific residual.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Rank sweep: routing benefit at each capacity level\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Rank\", \"MB Total Params\", \"Meta-Brain\", \"Univ (matched)\", \"Routing Benefit\", \"p-value\"]\n          - [\"16\", \"~12M\", \"63.8 ± 2.1\", \"62.4 ± 2.0\", \"+1.4\", \"0.18\"]\n          - [\"32\", \"~24M\", \"66.0 ± 1.8\", \"64.4 ± 1.8\", \"+1.6\", \"0.14\"]\n          - [\"64\", \"~48M\", \"68.4 ± 1.6\", \"66.2 ± 1.6\", \"+2.2\", \"0.08\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Line plot: x=expert rank (log scale), y=routing benefit (pp). Near-flat line with slight upward trend, all points below the 5pp significance threshold. Extrapolation dashed line shows impractical rank needed for meaningful benefit.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The rank sweep is the definitive nail: routing benefit does not emerge at any tested\n      capacity. The worst-case dream hypothesis predicted no capacity threshold rescue —\n      confirmed. Even at r=64 with 48M total params (4× the original budget), routing adds\n      only 2.2pp (p=0.08). The trend is real but economically irrational: spending 4×\n      the parameter budget for a 0.8pp improvement in routing benefit. Frame in the paper\n      as: \"Routing benefit grows sub-linearly with expert capacity, suggesting that\n      the correction subspace learned by LoRA experts is dominated by a shared\n      perturbation-agnostic component at all tested ranks.\"\n\n  - name: \"Clean LIBERO evaluation — nominal performance preservation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_015\"\n    description: >\n      Evaluate Meta-Brain and baselines on standard (unperturbed) LIBERO to verify routing\n      does not degrade nominal performance.\n    expected_results:\n      frozen_clean: 92.0\n      universal_r16_clean: 90.4\n      universal_r48_clean: 89.6\n      random_routing_clean: 88.8\n      meta_brain_clean: 87.8\n      oracle_clean: 88.2\n      meta_brain_clean_delta: -4.2\n      r48_clean_delta: -2.4\n      r16_clean_delta: -1.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      Meta-Brain degrades clean LIBERO by -4.2pp (92.0% → 87.8%), the largest degradation\n      among all methods. This occurs because the routing mechanism CANNOT detect \"no\n      perturbation\" as a distinct state — PAR was trained on 3-class perturbation classification\n      and always routes to some expert. On clean episodes, LoRA corrections are actively\n      harmful: they adjust representations that were already correct, introducing noise into\n      the action predictions. The degradation hierarchy (r16: -1.6pp, r48: -2.4pp,\n      Meta-Brain: -4.2pp) correlates with intervention magnitude — more LoRA correction =\n      more damage on clean inputs. Oracle routing (88.2%) also degrades because it still\n      applies expert corrections (just the \"correct\" one for the predicted type), and no\n      expert is correct for clean inputs. Notably, random routing (88.8%) degrades LESS\n      than Meta-Brain (87.8%), suggesting that Meta-Brain's near-uniform soft routing\n      distributes correction noise more uniformly across layers than random hard routing's\n      single-expert corrections. The -4.2pp clean degradation is a significant practical\n      concern: a deployment that encounters a mix of perturbed and clean episodes would\n      see Meta-Brain's net benefit diminished. If 50% of deployment episodes are clean,\n      the net effect is (+10.2 × 0.5) + (-4.2 × 0.5) = +3.0pp — barely better than\n      rank-16 universal's +5.6pp gain with negligible clean degradation.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Clean LIBERO performance\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Method\", \"Clean LIBERO (%)\", \"Δ vs Frozen\", \"LIBERO-Plus (%)\", \"Net (50/50 mix)\"]\n          - [\"Frozen pi0\", \"92.0\", \"—\", \"53.6\", \"72.8\"]\n          - [\"Universal r16\", \"90.4\", \"-1.6\", \"59.2\", \"74.8\"]\n          - [\"Universal r48\", \"89.6\", \"-2.4\", \"62.4\", \"76.0\"]\n          - [\"Random routing\", \"88.8\", \"-3.2\", \"63.5\", \"76.2\"]\n          - [\"Meta-Brain\", \"87.8\", \"-4.2\", \"63.8\", \"75.8\"]\n          - [\"Oracle\", \"88.2\", \"-3.8\", \"68.8\", \"78.5\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dual-axis bar chart: LIBERO-Plus gain (up) vs Clean LIBERO loss (down) per method, showing Meta-Brain's unfavorable gain/loss ratio compared to simpler baselines.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The clean degradation story is damaging for deployment. The 50/50 mix column reveals\n      that Universal r48 (76.0%) actually outperforms Meta-Brain (75.8%) in realistic\n      mixed scenarios, because r48's smaller clean degradation (-2.4pp vs -4.2pp) offsets\n      its slightly lower LIBERO-Plus performance. A reviewer focused on practical deployment\n      would prefer r48 + its simplicity over Meta-Brain + routing overhead. The paper should\n      acknowledge this limitation honestly and suggest a perturbation DETECTION gate (using\n      Phase 0 binary classifier) to disable LoRA corrections on clean inputs — but this\n      adds yet another module and further undermines the elegance of the routing approach.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-backbone validation — SmolVLA\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Replicate Phase 0 + Phase 1 core pipeline on SmolVLA to test generalizability of\n      perturbation-diagnostic routing across architectures.\n    expected_results:\n      smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.968\n      smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n      smolvla_phase0_k1_f1: 0.91\n      smolvla_frozen_libero_plus: 48.2\n      smolvla_universal_r16_overall: 54.0\n      smolvla_meta_brain_overall: 55.2\n      smolvla_routing_gap_vs_universal: 1.2\n      smolvla_random_routing_overall: 54.8\n      smolvla_routing_gap_vs_random: 0.4\n      smolvla_total_gpu_hours: 28\n    result_rationale: >\n      Phase 0 transfers well to SmolVLA (3-class F1=0.968), confirming the diagnostic\n      finding is architecture-general: VLA internal representations encode perturbation type\n      regardless of backbone. The best classification layer shifts (layer 4 vs pi0's layer 6)\n      due to SmolVLA's different depth, but the single-layer, single-token classification\n      property holds. However, Phase 1 routing shows the same failure pattern: Meta-Brain\n      (55.2%) only marginally outperforms universal r16 (54.0%, gap=1.2pp) and random\n      routing (54.8%, gap=0.4pp). The routing benefit is even smaller than on pi0 (1.2pp\n      vs 1.4pp), consistent with SmolVLA's smaller model having less capacity for expert\n      specialization. This cross-backbone result strengthens the Phase 0 finding (diagnostic\n      signal is universal) while simultaneously confirming the Phase 1 negative result\n      (routing doesn't help) generalizes across architectures.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"SmolVLA cross-backbone summary\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Metric\", \"pi0\", \"SmolVLA\"]\n          - [\"Phase 0 3-class F1\", \"0.993\", \"0.968\"]\n          - [\"Phase 0 best layer K=1\", \"layer 6 (F1=1.0)\", \"layer 4 (F1=0.91)\"]\n          - [\"Frozen baseline\", \"53.6%\", \"48.2%\"]\n          - [\"MB vs Universal r16 gap\", \"+4.6pp\", \"+1.2pp\"]\n          - [\"MB vs Random gap\", \"+0.3pp\", \"+0.4pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side ablation ladders for pi0 and SmolVLA, showing parallel structure: Phase 0 succeeds, Phase 1 routing fails on both.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-backbone validation is the strongest result for the reframed paper. Phase 0\n      generalizes cleanly (0.968 F1 on SmolVLA), while Phase 1 fails consistently — this\n      supports reframing as \"VLA internals encode perturbation type (generalizable finding)\n      but routing-based correction doesn't exploit it (negative result).\" Use SmolVLA data\n      to strengthen Phase 0 as the primary contribution and present routing failure as\n      architecture-independent, not pi0-specific.\n\n  - name: \"LoRA layer placement ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA experts: early (2-6), mid (6-12), late\n      (12-16), all, and matched placement (V→early, S→mid, P→late).\n    expected_results:\n      all_layers_overall: 63.8\n      early_only_overall: 62.2\n      mid_only_overall: 62.8\n      late_only_overall: 61.8\n      matched_placement_overall: 63.2\n      matched_vs_all_gap: -0.6\n      early_visual: 65.0\n      mid_visual: 64.0\n      late_visual: 63.0\n      early_physical: 53.0\n      mid_physical: 55.0\n      late_physical: 56.0\n      matched_visual: 65.4\n      matched_spatial: 60.0\n      matched_physical: 56.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Layer placement shows weak effects (1.6pp spread across configurations). All-layers\n      (63.8%) is best, and matched placement (63.2%) does NOT outperform it, disconfirming\n      the hypothesis that V→early, S→mid, P→late mapping from Phase 0 translates to\n      correction benefit. The matched placement shows mild per-type trends consistent with\n      Phase 0 (early layers slightly better for visual, late for physical), but the effects\n      are within noise. This occurs because: (1) Phase 0's layer mapping describes WHERE\n      perturbation information is encoded, not WHERE corrections should be applied — these\n      are different properties; (2) LoRA corrections propagate through subsequent layers\n      regardless of injection point, so early-layer LoRA eventually affects late-layer\n      representations; (3) the dominant correction signal is distributed across all layers,\n      not localized. The all-layers configuration wins simply by having more parameters\n      applied (72 LoRA modules across 18 layers vs ~24 modules for partial-layer configs),\n      consistent with the capacity-over-specialization theme.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Layer placement ablation\"\n        rows:\n          - [\"Config\", \"Layers\", \"LoRA modules\", \"Visual\", \"Spatial\", \"Physical\", \"Overall\"]\n          - [\"Early only\", \"2-6\", \"20\", \"65.0\", \"57.0\", \"53.0\", \"62.2\"]\n          - [\"Mid only\", \"6-12\", \"28\", \"64.0\", \"59.0\", \"55.0\", \"62.8\"]\n          - [\"Late only\", \"12-16\", \"20\", \"63.0\", \"58.0\", \"56.0\", \"61.8\"]\n          - [\"Matched (V→E,S→M,P→L)\", \"mixed\", \"~24\", \"65.4\", \"60.0\", \"56.8\", \"63.2\"]\n          - [\"All layers\", \"2-16\", \"72\", \"67.1\", \"59.2\", \"55.2\", \"63.8\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Layer × perturbation-type heatmap showing mild interaction effects that are within noise of all-layer baseline.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The layer placement ablation provides a nuanced finding for the reframed paper:\n      Phase 0's layer-perturbation mapping (V→early, S→mid, P→late) describes diagnostic\n      encoding, not optimal correction sites. Frame as: \"The mechanistic insight about\n      where perturbation information is encoded does not straightforwardly translate to\n      where corrections should be applied — full-layer coverage remains optimal, suggesting\n      correction requires global representation adjustment rather than targeted layer\n      intervention.\" This is an interesting negative result that adds depth to the\n      mechanistic analysis without overpromising.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"cd55bb73-8a31-4b84-9f66-dba77b234ae1","total_cost_usd":2.277615,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":78492,"cache_read_input_tokens":37492,"output_tokens":70360,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":78492,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":6360,"cache_read_input_tokens":26045,"cache_creation_input_tokens":63894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":63894},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":9149,"outputTokens":24,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.009269,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":5,"outputTokens":70360,"cacheReadInputTokens":37492,"cacheCreationInputTokens":78492,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.268346,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"fddffcc9-2a4d-4a95-a888-2599ff91c224"}
